<header class="start-masthead"><p class="eyebrow">Lab runtime · proposed documentation</p><h1>Recover and retry safely</h1><p>Distinguish an absent effect, a committed outcome and uncertainty before repeating work.</p><p class="source-note">Draft prepared on 2026-10-14. Integration and final T21 qualification remain pending.</p></header>

<section id="runtime-recovery-select-the-state-you-are-recovering" aria-labelledby="runtime-recovery-select-the-state-you-are-recovering-title"><h2 id="runtime-recovery-select-the-state-you-are-recovering-title">Select the state you are recovering</h2>

<p>A v1 store recovers staged local transactions around an atomic HEAD commit. The API Vault journals candidate operation claims/material/results. Action has its own tool-effect/reconciliation boundary; Governance owns separate native commits. SQLite projections, exports and managed files are derived or external publications with their own guards.</p>

<p>Keep each store identity, owner, source cut, exact request and effect boundary. A timeout or missing final response does not prove no effect. Source/profile mismatches and expired permission do not authorize inspection of foreign private state.</p>

</section>

<section id="runtime-recovery-follow-v1-head-recovery" aria-labelledby="runtime-recovery-follow-v1-head-recovery-title"><h2 id="runtime-recovery-follow-v1-head-recovery-title">Follow v1 HEAD recovery</h2>

<p>Read the selected store&#x27;s status under current scope, then verify its committed chain. recover requires --expected-head with the exact digest from that store; do not substitute this handbook&#x27;s D74 repository-inspection digest. The <a href="research/sources/implementation/lab-runtime/v1/lab_runtime/store.py">original Store recovery</a> preserves committed transactions and removes only recognized pending/staging material or an immediate uncommitted next transaction.</p>

<p>Unknown future transactions remain for inspection. Controlled process interruption on the observed local filesystem is the supported verification scope. Power-loss durability and multi-host consensus are not established. Rebuild a disposable graph only after source/HEAD freshness; a graph file cannot become replay authority.</p>

</section>

<section id="runtime-recovery-handle-api-idempotency-and-pending-effects" aria-labelledby="runtime-recovery-handle-api-idempotency-and-pending-effects-title"><h2 id="runtime-recovery-handle-api-idempotency-and-pending-effects-title">Handle API idempotency and pending effects</h2>

<p>The exact key belongs to an authenticated Principal/authority/scope/operation namespace. Keep the original control-input fingerprint. Same-key changed bytes conflict. Completed retries return or locate their guarded historical result; pending or uncertain keys request inspection/reconciliation and do not automatically redispatch.</p>

<p>The <a href="research/sources/product/lab/release-candidates/v1/interface/api-integration-v16/workspace.py">captured Vault claim code</a> looks up prior state under the original writer lock before fresh allocation. The reviewed margin gate belongs only to a new fresh application/assessment claim; it must not make an old recorded outcome depend on a new Session. Original source, authorization, cut, deadline and journal guards still apply.</p>

<figure id="recover-effect"><pre><code class="language-mermaid">flowchart TD
  Q[Exact request and key] --&gt; J[Inspect owned journal]
  J --&gt;|Completed| R[Read guarded recorded result]
  J --&gt;|Pending / uncertain| I[Operation inspection]
  I --&gt; A[Authorized reconciliation]
  A --&gt; C[Cold replay / exact observed outcome]
  J --&gt;|Fresh and authorized| G[Current pre-effect guards]
  G --&gt; E[Effect and durable evidence]
  E --&gt; C</code></pre><figcaption>Completed outcomes are read as history. Pending or uncertain outcomes require current owned inspection and authorized reconciliation before a new effect. A fresh request follows current guards, then records observed effects. The diagram does not authorize redispatch.</figcaption><p>Explanatory component/sequence map, not a declaration of new ontology relation kinds or qualified runtime execution.</p></figure>

</section>

<section id="runtime-recovery-preserve-committed-work-when-derived-output-fails" aria-labelledby="runtime-recovery-preserve-committed-work-when-derived-output-fails-title"><h2 id="runtime-recovery-preserve-committed-work-when-derived-output-fails-title">Preserve committed work when derived output fails</h2>

<p>A committed native transaction stays committed if a later projection/export/file generation fails. Reconcile the selected output boundary instead of applying the transaction again. The <a href="research/sources/docs/plans/0011-lab-runtime-completion/runs/0001-execution/T14/mixed-target-publisher-0002/README.md">mixed-target publisher successor</a> stages ordinary filesystem/receipt behavior; its scoped controls are not proof of every native mixed-target admission.</p>

<p>The capture4 writer also replaces several metadata files source-last without a cross-file transaction. An interrupted replacement can leave mixed generations. Its reviewer flags that standing; Root must inspect and use exact retained preimages through a separately reviewed recovery. Do not call source-last publication atomic across files.</p>

</section>

<section id="runtime-recovery-what-to-retain" aria-labelledby="runtime-recovery-what-to-retain-title"><h2 id="runtime-recovery-what-to-retain-title">What to retain</h2>

<p>Retain permitted public request/result digests, exact selected version, observed phase/terminal, pending/committed posture, current authority scope and uncertainty. Never log private proof/key bodies or Session notes. Preserve unknown residue and failed candidates. Cleanup removes only exactly owned disposable objects whose retention decision is established.</p>

<p>The integrated full positive/security/recovery/native qualification is still pending. Use <a href="runtime-status.html">the dated evidence</a> to tell an implemented recovery path from a current successful witness.</p>

</section>

